    "# 2. Strategy Construction: CPI MoM Surprise \u2192 t+1 Long/Short\n",
    "\n",
    "We use CPI-only MoM surprise to construct a one-day-ahead trading strategy on SPY and QQQ.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Signal rule (example specification):**\n",
    "\n",
    "Let\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "- If Surprise\\_t > 0: **short** ETF at t+1 \u2192 signal = \u22121  \n",
    "- If Surprise\\_t < 0: **long** ETF at t+1 \u2192 signal = +1  \n",
    "- If Surprise\\_t = 0: **no position** \u2192 signal = 0  \n",
    "\n",
    "Each CPI release date is mapped to the **next trading day** (`trade_date`), and the\n",
    "signal is applied only on that day\u2019s return.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "xC2oGeZfETSJ"
   },
   "source": [
    "## Assumption Test"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {
    "id": "k5IUIniFIPrj"
   },
   "source": [
    "Prior to implementing the trading strategy and conducting factor regressions, several diagnostic tests are performed to assess key modeling assumptions. First, the autocorrelation function (ACF) of the CPI MoM surprise series indicates strong first-order persistence but minimal correlation at higher lags, justifying the construction of a momentum-based macro signal.\n",
    "\n",
    "Second, the Jarque\u2013Bera test strongly rejects the null of normality for daily SPY returns, with substantial skewness and excess kurtosis observed. This confirms the presence of fat tails and non-Gaussian behavior, which is a well-documented feature of financial return data. Accordingly, all statistical inference in subsequent regressions is conducted using heteroskedasticity- and autocorrelation-consistent (HAC) standard errors.\n"
   ]
  },
